{% extends 'legal/_layout.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Privacy · {{ company.trading_name }}{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}How Kaltra handles practice and patient data.{% endblock %}

{% block doc_title %}Privacy{% endblock %}
{% block doc_subtitle %}
    Version {{ version }}. Covers kaltra.cz and the service itself.
    The Czech text is the binding one; this is a translation for convenience.
{% endblock %}

{% block doc_body %}
    <p>
        This service is operated by <strong>{{ company.legal_name }}</strong>, company
        no. {{ company.company_number }}, registered at {{ company.street }},
        {{ company.postcode }} {{ company.city }}, Czech Republic. For data protection
        matters: <a href="mailto:{{ company.email }}">{{ company.email }}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Two different roles</h2>

    <p>
        We handle two kinds of data and our role differs for each. That distinction
        governs the whole document, so it comes first.
    </p>

    <h3>Patient data — we are the processor</h3>
    <p>
        Patient data is uploaded by the practice. <strong>The practice is the
        controller</strong>; we are only the processor and act on its instructions.
        We do not decide whose data is processed or why. The terms are in the
        processing agreement the practice accepts before uploading anything.
    </p>

    <h3>Practice and staff data — we are the controller</h3>
    <p>
        For the sign-in and billing details of the people who use the service, we are
        the controller.
    </p>

    <h2>What patient data we hold</h2>

    <p>From the export a practice uploads, we store only:</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>The practice's own patient identifier</td><td>Our only handle on a person.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Phone number</td><td>Where the invitation goes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Surname (optional)</td><td>So the doctor can recognise who they are approving. The practice need not send it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Health insurer</td><td>Decides how long before the insurer will pay for the examination again.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Date of the last preventive examination and dispensary review</td><td>The due date is calculated from these.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Recall group name</td><td>For example "Diabetes". <strong>Never a diagnosis code.</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Diagnoses</h3>
    <p>
        Where an export carries a diagnosis column, it is read <strong>while the file
        is being processed</strong>, to work out who is under dispensary care and how
        often they are due. The code is never written to the database. What stays
        against the patient is the name of the recall group the practice chose — so
        "Diabetes", never "E11.8". The uploaded file is deleted once processed.
    </p>

    <h3>What we do not want</h3>
    <p>
        Birth numbers, medical records, test results, or anything beyond the table
        above. Where an export contains such columns, we neither read nor store them.
    </p>

    <h2>What the SMS says</h2>
    <p>
        The message contains no name, no diagnosis, no date and no reason for the
        visit. If a number in an export is wrong, the worst case is that a stranger
        receives a vague invitation — not that they learn something about someone
        else's health. The sender shown is the practice's name, not our company.
    </p>

    <h2>Legal basis</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Patient data:</strong> processed under the processing agreement with the practice (Art. 28 GDPR). The legal basis for the processing itself is determined by the practice as controller.</li>
        <li><strong>Account and sign-in:</strong> performance of a contract (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR).</li>
        <li><strong>Invoicing and accounting:</strong> compliance with a legal obligation (Art. 6(1)(c) GDPR).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who else is involved</h2>

    <p>We do not sell data to anyone. These processors take part in the service:</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Who</th><th>For what</th><th>What reaches them</th><th>Where</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>46elks AB</td>
                <td>Sending SMS</td>
                <td>The patient's phone number and the message text</td>
                <td>Sweden (EU)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Stripe</td>
                <td>Card payments</td>
                <td>The practice's billing details and card. <strong>No patient data.</strong></td>
                <td>EU/US</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Hetzner Online GmbH</td>
                <td>Running the server and database</td>
                <td>Everything stored in the service</td>
                <td>Germany (EU)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Cloudflare, Inc.</td>
                <td>Site protection, connection encryption and content delivery</td>
                <td>All traffic between the practice and the service, including the uploaded file</td>
                <td>US / global network</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Simple Analytics B.V.</td>
                <td>Website traffic</td>
                <td>The page visited, where the visitor came from, device type and country. No patient data, no visitor profile.</td>
                <td>Netherlands (EU)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Mailtrap</td>
                <td>Emails to the practice (sign-in codes, invoices)</td>
                <td>A staff email address. No patient data.</td>
                <td>US (Standard Contractual Clauses)</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        Stored patient data is held in Germany. Stripe is a processor <strong>for
        payment data only</strong> and never receives patient data.
    </p>

    <h3>Transfers outside the EU</h3>
    <p>
        Traffic to the site passes through Cloudflare's network, which protects the
        service against attack and encrypts the connection. In doing so Cloudflare
        processes the data in transit — including the file a practice uploads — and
        is a company established in the United States. The transfer is covered by
        Standard Contractual Clauses under Art. 46 GDPR within our data processing
        agreement with Cloudflare.
    </p>
    <p>
        Cloudflare does not <strong>store</strong> that data in this arrangement; it
        passes through while a request is served. Data at rest stays on servers in
        Germany.
    </p>

    <h2>How long we keep things</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The uploaded file:</strong> deleted as soon as it is processed, usually within seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Patient data:</strong> for the life of the agreement with the practice. Deleted on request once it ends.</li>
        <li><strong>The record of a sent SMS:</strong> for the life of the agreement, so the practice can see who received one.</li>
        <li><strong>Invoices and accounting records:</strong> for the period the law requires — tax documents 10 years, other accounting records 5. This cannot be shortened on request.</li>
        <li><strong>Account and sign-in data:</strong> for the life of the account.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
        We use <strong>strictly necessary cookies only</strong>: one for signing in
        and form security, and Cloudflare's security cookie (<code>__cf_bm</code>),
        which distinguishes a person from automated traffic.
    </p>
    <p>
        No advertising, no tracking, and nothing is shared with ad networks. That
        is why we do not ask you about cookies — strictly necessary ones require
        no consent.
    </p>

    <h2>Website measurement</h2>
    <p>
        We measure site traffic with Simple Analytics. It
        <strong>stores nothing on your device</strong> — no cookie, no
        <code>localStorage</code>, no browser fingerprint. No consent is required
        for that reason: the obligation to ask attaches to storing information on
        your device or reading what is already there, and neither happens here.
    </p>
    <p>
        We measure aggregate figures only: which page was visited, where the
        visitor came from, device type and country. We build no visitor profile
        and do not follow anyone across sites. Simple Analytics processes your IP
        address only to derive the country and does not store it. The legal basis
        is our legitimate interest in knowing which pages help people
        (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR); you may object to that processing.
    </p>
    <p>
        The site's typeface is self-hosted. Loading a font from a third party
        would send your IP address to its operator on every page view; that does
        not happen here.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
        You have the right of access, rectification, erasure, restriction of
        processing, portability, and the right to object.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>If you are a patient</strong>, please contact your practice — they are
        the controller of your data and we act on their instructions. If you write to
        us directly, we will pass the request on.
    </p>
    <p>
        You may complain to the Czech data protection authority: Úřad pro ochranu
        osobních údajů, Pplk. Sochora 27, 170 00 Praha 7,
        <a href="https://uoou.gov.cz">uoou.gov.cz</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>All traffic is encrypted over HTTPS.</li>
        <li>Sign-in is by one-time code rather than a password — there is nothing to steal or reuse.</li>
        <li>A practice sees only its own patients; access is constrained at the query level.</li>
        <li>Uploaded files are staged outside the web root and deleted once read.</li>
        <li>Birth numbers, where they appear in an insurance claim file, are hashed before anything is stored and never written down in their original form.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
        We may revise this document. Material changes are announced to practices by
        email at least 30 days in advance. The version is shown at the top.
    </p>
{% endblock %}

{% block doc_footer %}
    {{ company.legal_name }}, company no. {{ company.company_number }},
    {{ company.street }}, {{ company.postcode }} {{ company.city }} ·
    <a href="mailto:{{ company.email }}">{{ company.email }}</a>
{% endblock %}
